    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">221 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the assassination of Hasdrubal, Hannibal is proclaimed commander-in-chief by the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> at the age of 26</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">218 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a> in late spring, crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He destroys the army of the consul Gaius Flaminius in an ambush on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">216 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> he surrounds a Roman army, of which 50,000–70,000 are estimated killed or captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">211 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He marches on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to draw off the besiegers of Capua, but the city falls to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">203 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After nearly 15 years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he is recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> to defend it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">202 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Scipio defeats him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a>, the last major battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">190 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Commanding a Seleucid fleet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, he is forced to retreat by the Rhodian navy at the Battle of Side</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca.jpg" alt="Hamilcar Barca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amilcare_Barca_(Ritratto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Max Karl Baldamus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hannibal was born in 247 BCE, most likely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, one of the sons of the Carthaginian general and statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>; his mother is unknown. He had two brothers, Hasdrubal and Mago, and several sisters whose names are not recorded; his brothers-in-law were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> and the Numidian king Naravas. Barca, meaning "lightning" or "thunderbolt", was a surname his father acquired for the swiftness and ferocity of his attacks.</p><p>He grew up in the aftermath of Carthage's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>. Hamilcar set out to restore his family's and Carthage's fortunes by subduing the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Carthage was then so poor that it had no navy able to carry his army, and he marched it across Numidia to the Pillars of Hercules. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Hannibal said much later that when he begged to go with his father, Hamilcar agreed and made him swear that he would never be a friend of Rome as long as he lived. One account sets the oath at the age of nine, with Hamilcar holding the boy over a sacrificial fire; tradition places it at Peñíscola.</p><p>When Hamilcar drowned in battle, Hannibal's brother-in-law Hasdrubal the Fair succeeded to the command, and Hannibal, then 18, served under him as an officer. Hasdrubal consolidated Carthage's Iberian interests and signed a treaty with Rome by which Carthage would not expand north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> so long as Rome did not expand south of it. Hannibal's view of military affairs came partly from his Greek tutors and partly from experience gained beside his father.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the assassination of Hasdrubal in 221 BCE Hannibal, aged 26, was proclaimed commander-in-chief by the army and confirmed by the Carthaginian government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> writes that the old soldiers "fancied they saw Hamilcar in his youth given back to them". Livy also records that he married a woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a>, a powerful Spanish city allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><p>He spent two years completing the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>. He stormed Alithia, the strongest centre of the Olcades, and in 220 BCE the Vaccaean strongholds of Helmantice and Arbucala; on the way home he defeated a coalition of tribes led by the Carpetani at the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, his first major success in the field. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, fearing his growing strength, made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, well south of the Ebro. Hannibal took this as a breach of the treaty and, since he was already planning an attack on Rome, besieged the city, which fell after eight months. He sent the booty to Carthage, where, by Livy's record, only Hanno II the Great spoke against him. A Roman delegation led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a> Verrucosus demanded that Carthage choose between war and peace; told that Rome could choose, Fabius chose war.</p><p>Hannibal left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a> in late spring 218 BCE and reportedly entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with 40,000 foot soldiers and 12,000 horsemen. He reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> by September, with 38 elephants, and evaded a Roman force by turning inland. His route over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> has been disputed ever since. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with 20,000 foot soldiers, 6,000 horsemen and only a few elephants.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cannae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Death_of_Paulus_Aemilius_at_the_Battle_of_Cannae_(Yale_University_Art_Gallery_scan).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hannibal's appearance in the Po Valley detached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In November 218 BCE his cavalry beat the consul Publius Cornelius Scipio at the Ticinus, a minor victory that brought Gauls and Ligurians to his side and raised his army to around 40,000 men. In December, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, he wore down the Roman infantry and then cut it to pieces with an ambush from the flanks. In the spring of 217 BCE he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the flooded marshes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, where he lost a large part of his force and the sight of his right eye. By marching around the consul Gaius Flaminius and cutting him off from Rome he drew him into a defile on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and destroyed his army; Flaminius was killed.</p><p>In 216 BCE he seized the supply depot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> raised an army estimated by some at 100,000 men but more likely 50,000–80,000. Hannibal placed his least reliable infantry forward in the centre, let it give way, and closed on the Romans with his Libyan infantry from the wings and his cavalry from behind. It is estimated that 50,000–70,000 Romans were killed or captured, among them the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> and 80 senators.</p><p>After Cannae Capua, the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, came over to him and became his base, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> pledged his support. He occupied most of southern Italy for 15 years. In 212 BCE he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> and destroyed two Roman armies, with 15,000 and 16,000 Romans killed, and in 208 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus-consul-166-bce">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a> fell in battle against him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="Cicero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Cicero_(1st-cent._BC)_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s answer was the strategy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a>, appointed dictator after Trasimene, who avoided open battle and shadowed Hannibal's army; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> kept to it, wearing him down with several smaller armies. The Romans refused all overtures for peace and would not ransom the prisoners of Cannae. Only a few of the Italian cities he had hoped to win defected to him, and without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> he could not take Rome; his cavalry commander Maharbal is quoted as saying, "Hannibal, you know how to gain a victory, but not how to use one." Capua fell in 211 BCE despite his march on Rome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> was lost in 209 BCE, and in 207 BCE his brother Hasdrubal was defeated and killed at the Metaurus; his head was thrown into Hannibal's camp.</p><p>He was opposed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> too. The peace party of Hanno II the Great, whose wealth rested on trade with Rome, resented the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a> as unauthorised and was instrumental in denying him reinforcements after Cannae. Requests for supplies and siege equipment were delayed or ignored.</p><p>Most of what is known of him comes from Roman sources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says that he was extremely cruel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> set the "cruel" Hannibal beside the "honourable" Pyrrhus. Yet he is also recorded as searching for the body of Flaminius and sending the ashes of Marcellus to his family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, whom some historians judge a partisan witness, says only that the Romans accused him of cruelty and the Carthaginians of avarice, and suggests that he may have been confused with one of his officers, Hannibal Monomachus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus.jpg" alt="Scipio Africanus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(loan_from_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek)_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 203 BCE, after nearly 15 years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Hannibal was recalled to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> against a Roman invasion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>. At Zama the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, joined by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> of Numidia, were superior in cavalry, while Hannibal had more men and 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. The Roman cavalry routed the Carthaginian and returned to attack his rear, and his formation collapsed. Carthage lost approximately 20,000 troops, with 15,000 more wounded.</p><p>The peace left Carthage with an indemnity of ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. Hannibal, 46 at the end of the war in 201 BCE, was elected suffete, the chief magistrate. He reorganised the state finances to eliminate corruption and recover embezzled funds, and had the judges of the Hundred and Four chosen by direct election for a term of one year instead of for life. Seven years after Zama the Romans, alarmed by Carthage's prosperity and suspecting him of contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, sent a delegation to Carthage. Aware that his reforms had made him many enemies, he fled before his surrender could be demanded.</p><p>He went to the court of Antiochus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. In 190 BCE, commanding a fleet he had built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, he was beaten by the Rhodians at Side. When Antiochus, defeated at Magnesia, promised to hand him over, he fled again, and finally served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prusias-i-of-bithynia">Prusias I of Bithynia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>. The Romans pressed Prusias to give him up. Cornelius Nepos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> say that he took poison when soldiers surrounded his house; Pausanias and Appian tell it differently. His death is placed between 183 and 181 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/libyssa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/libyssa.jpg" alt="Libyssa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/libyssa">Libyssa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanibal%27s_Tomb_(7471635270).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Neuheisel from DC, US, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hannibal became such a figure of terror at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that in moments of danger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> would exclaim "Hannibal ad portas", "Hannibal is at the gates", a phrase still in use. Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Frontinus and Juvenal show a grudging admiration for him, and the Romans are said to have set up statues of him in their streets to advertise the defeat of so worthy an adversary. The historian John Lazenby stresses that Rome's government went on working through the disasters, without revolutions, peace factions or coups, and the war has been called Rome's "finest hour". In the thirteenth century the Byzantine scholar John Tzetzes wrote that Severus, likely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, who was of Libyan birth, built a tomb of white marble for Hannibal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyssa">Libyssa</a>.</p><p>He is generally regarded as one of the best military strategists and tacticians of all time, and military academies still study his campaigns, above all the double envelopment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>. Alfred von Schlieffen drew on that envelopment in developing his "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>" before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Appian tells of a meeting at which Hannibal named Alexander and Pyrrhus as the greatest generals and himself third, adding that had he beaten Scipio he would have put himself before Alexander.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> he is widely revered as a national hero: his likeness has appeared on five-dinar notes issued in 1993 and 2013, and a station on the TGM railway line is named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> Hannibal. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> a cenotaph was built in his memory in 1981 south of present-day Gebze, as an expression of Atatürk's respect for him.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hannibal, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, led a Carthaginian army over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in 218 BCE and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, but could not end the war. Beaten at Zama in 202 BCE, he reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> as suffete, fled into exile and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> between 183 and 181 BCE.</em></p>

